WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.000
THE TRAITOROUS EIGHT:
These 8 backstabbed their professor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:09.720
and created their own company using his research.&nbsp;
While that sounds pretty bad, they weren’t exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:14.460
trying to screw over their professor. They&nbsp;
just wanted freedom and the ability to lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:19.800
their own company. Little did they know that&nbsp;
this betrayal would end up being the foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:26.760
of not just Silicon Valley but modern tech as a&nbsp;
whole. Why you ask? Well, these were the men who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:33.180
commercialized semiconductor technology or silicon&nbsp;
wafers hence the name silicon valley. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:39.480
you know how there’s a bunch of debate regarding&nbsp;
who’s the better chip maker AMD or Intel? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:46.260
these guys created both. In fact, they also played&nbsp;
integral roles in the creation of Apple, Google,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:52.380
Oracle, Facebook, Cisco, Applied Materials,&nbsp;
Nvidia, Juniper Networks, Sun Microsystems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:58.860
Netscape, and all of these other companies as&nbsp;
well just to name a few. But, despite their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:04.320
revolutionary contribution to the tech scene,&nbsp;
unless you’re a tech scholar, you’ve probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:09.960
never even heard of them. I mean, have you ever&nbsp;
heard of Sheldon Roberts or Eugene Kleiner or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:15.720
Julius Blank? Or what about their professor&nbsp;
William Shockley? I’m gonna guess probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:22.440
not. So, here’s the insane untold story of the&nbsp;
traitorous 8 and the creation of Silicon Valley.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:27.480
THE SILICON MAN:&nbsp;

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:36.300
Taking a look back, the story of Silicon Valley&nbsp;
can be traced back to a man named William Bradford&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:42.960
Shockley Jr. He was born on February 13, 1910 in&nbsp;
London, but most of his childhood would be spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:49.020
in the family’s hometown of Palo Alto, California.&nbsp;
Shockley didn’t have the strongest of backgrounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:54.060
but he definitely had grit engrained in his&nbsp;
blood. His father worked the dangerous job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:58.920
of being a mining engineer and his mother&nbsp;
followed in the same foot steps. Fun fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:04.860
his mother would became America’s first female&nbsp;
deputy mining surveyor. So, these guys were very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:11.160
much about the no pain no gain mentality and they&nbsp;
expected nothing less from Shockley Jr. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:16.020
they would recruit a Stanford profesor who lived&nbsp;
nearby to teach Shockley physics at a very young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:22.440
age. Clearly, getting Shockley educated was a top&nbsp;
priority for his parents, but they weren’t exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:28.380
fans of the public school system. They actually&nbsp;
particularly hated public schools and this was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:33.240
main reason that Shockley was homeschooled until&nbsp;
the age of 8. This also played well with the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:38.280
that Shockley regularly had violent tantrums&nbsp;
and didn’t get along with other children all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:44.160
that well. It looks like this was something that&nbsp;
Shockley never really left behind, but anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:49.200
it looks like Shockley more than fulfilled his&nbsp;
parent’s dream as he didn’t just get educated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:55.200
he became a genius. He would graduate from&nbsp;
Caltech in 1932 with a bachelors and MIT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:00.660
in 1936 with a PhD. And after graduation,&nbsp;
he would turn around and join the highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:06.660
prestigious Bell Labs. If you don’t know about&nbsp;
Bell Labs, think Google Moonshot factory but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:12.240
100 years ago. Shockley stuck around here for&nbsp;
a couple of years until WW2 broke out at which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:17.160
point he became a reserach director at Columbia&nbsp;
University. His job was originally to develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:22.920
radar technology that could detect submarines, but&nbsp;
he ended up becoming quite close to war leaders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:28.140
In fact, the War Department asked Shockley to&nbsp;
estimate the death toll of a certain explosion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:34.200
that marked the end of WW2. Anyway, Shockley&nbsp;
would return to Bell Labs following the war and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:40.140
begin reseraching transistors. Spoiler alert, this&nbsp;
research would end up creating modern technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:47.280
but it was by no means a fast or easy process.&nbsp;
In the early days, Shockley saw nothing but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:52.320
failure with his external electrical field&nbsp;
experiments on semiconductors. But, over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:57.840
Shockley and his colleagues were able to eliminate&nbsp;
one concern after another and they created the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:03.720
junction transistor in 1951. The creation of this&nbsp;
transistor was very much a team effort between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:09.960
Shockley, John Bardeen, and Walter Brattain. But,&nbsp;
the media often gave all the credit to Shockley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:15.480
given that he was the leader. Shockley would&nbsp;
go out of his to give credit to his partners&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:21.840
whenever possible. But Bardeen and Brattain still&nbsp;
felt pretty resentful, and they would both end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:28.800
leaving in the 1950s, but Shockley was just&nbsp;
getting started. In 1956, Shockley, Bardeen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:34.500
and Brattain would be awarded the Nobel Prize&nbsp;
for Physics. So, clearly, these guys were no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:40.440
doubt world class, but with Bardeen and Brattain&nbsp;
gone, Shockley needed a new team. This time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:46.200
he opted to recruit a bunch of PhD students&nbsp;
instead of already distinguished researchers. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:51.900
probably thought that he would have an easier time&nbsp;
managing students than peers. Oh how wrong he was.

00:04:51.900 --> 00:05:01.260
THE BETRAYAL:&nbsp;

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:07.140
Shockley would found Shockley Semiconductor&nbsp;
Laboratory in Mountain View, California in 1956.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:13.020
He would go onto recruit a bunch of PhD students&nbsp;
and recent grads to the company. Their goal was to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:18.420
make ground breaking discoveries regarding silicon&nbsp;
wafers. Let’s just say, they definitely ended up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:24.180
accomplishing this, but it was by no means smooth&nbsp;
sailing. You know how we talked about Shockley’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:29.280
violent tantrums as a child, well, these&nbsp;
characteristics were only magnified at Shockley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:35.640
Semiconductor, and the reason isn’t rocket&nbsp;
science. Shockley had literally won a nobel price&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:43.260
by the age of 46, so he felt like he was the daddy&nbsp;
of the semiconductors as whole. And technically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:50.520
he wasn’t wrong, but that by no means excuses his&nbsp;
behavior. Shockley has been described as the worst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:57.660
manager in the history of electronics. He was&nbsp;
autocratic, domineering, erratic, hard to please,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:04.860
and highly egotistical just to name a few traits.&nbsp;
But, to be honest, that’s not all that different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:11.400
from Steve Jobs or Elon Musk or Jeff Bezos or Bill&nbsp;
Gates or really any well known figure. Not being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:16.320
like this is really the exception when it comes&nbsp;
to figures like this. But, you could definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:21.660
say that Shockley took this to another level.&nbsp;
For example, all phone calls in the company were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:26.340
recorded, staff weren’t allowed to share results&nbsp;
with each other without Shockley’s permission,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:32.340
and Shockley even threatened to use lie detectors&nbsp;
at one point. This lack of trust wasn’t exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:36.840
the characteristics of a great boss, but what&nbsp;
really screwed over Shockley was his vision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:42.120
for the company. You see, 8 of Shockley’s best&nbsp;
researchers felt that the company should focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:48.480
on silicon base semiconductors and commercialize&nbsp;
the idea, but for some reason Shockley didn’t want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:54.600
to pursue this idea. This simply led to even&nbsp;
more disagreements and bad blood and it wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:59.760
long till reserachers started resigning. Most of&nbsp;
them would just go ahead and join laboratories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:05.340
and research teams in adjacent fields. But,&nbsp;
our traitorous eight didn’t want to pursue a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:11.220
different field. They could see that Shockley had&nbsp;
a winner, it just had to be executed correctly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:16.440
The group described it as solving the “Shockley&nbsp;
problem”. They’re first instinct wasn’t to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:21.180
leave though. They actually tried talking to the&nbsp;
investors of Shockley Semiconductor and asked them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:27.240
to reign in Shockley, but they weren’t exactly&nbsp;
onboard with this idea. So, our traitorous 8&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:32.280
decided to find their own investors in March&nbsp;
of 1957. The man who pulled the trigger was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:38.220
Eugene Kleiner. Kleiner was one of the few people&nbsp;
Shockley trusted, so when he requested a few days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:45.180
off to visit an exhibition in LA, Shockley didn’t&nbsp;
think twice. But, maybe he should’ve as Kleiner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:49.920
would end up traveling to New York instead and&nbsp;
pitching to investors. Finding an investor was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:55.620
actually super difficult. It’s not that investors&nbsp;
weren’t interested in the idea but they wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.620 --> 00:08:00.120
something that was closer to home. Trying to&nbsp;
manage these braniacs in California from New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:05.760
York seemed like a daunting task to say the least.&nbsp;
But, they eventually found someone who was willing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:12.180
to take the plunge: Sherman Fairchild. Sherman&nbsp;
agreed to loan our 8 protagonists $1.38 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:19.860
or the equivalent of $14.8 million today. And with&nbsp;
that, Fairchild semiconductor was established and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:24.660
the 8 geniuses from Shockley Semiconductor&nbsp;
would officially become the traitorous 8.

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:33.600
FAIRCHILD THE BRAINCHILD:
Our 8 would instantly get to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:38.580
work on commercializing silicon wafers. Their&nbsp;
first goal was to produce silicon wafers for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:43.800
digital devices based on the research of Bell&nbsp;
Labs and Shockley. I do gotta say though, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:48.720
didn’t rip off the tech completely. They would&nbsp;
actually try to improve it themselves to build an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:53.640
even bigger lead. They would set out on improving&nbsp;
the technology using three different methods each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:59.100
of which were led by one of the traitorous 8. The&nbsp;
method that ended up coming out on top was the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:05.580
that was led by Gordon Moore. If you’ve ever heard&nbsp;
of Moore’s law, well this is the guy. Fairchild&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:10.140
would go onto implement Moore’s discoveries&nbsp;
into Shockley’s research and within no time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:17.400
the company was mass producing silicon wafers.&nbsp;
And let’s just say, Fairchild destroyed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:23.940
competition. Throughout the early 1960s, they&nbsp;
were the undisputed king of semiconductors. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:29.220
obviously the company didn’t keep this lead&nbsp;
as they have since disappeared into oblivion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:35.220
so what happened? Well, it was the greed of&nbsp;
Sherman Fairchild. You see, one of the conditions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:39.240
of funding the company was that Sherman reserved&nbsp;
the right to buyout the stakes of the partners&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:45.420
at any time. Ideally, this clause was in place so&nbsp;
that Sherman could easily manage any bad actors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:51.120
If someone was causing issues and not being a team&nbsp;
player, well then, Sherman could just buy them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:57.480
out. But, the problem arose when Sherman started&nbsp;
buying out partners just because he could. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:03.780
no wonder why Sherman decided to do this. From his&nbsp;
perspective, Fairchild already had a massive lead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:09.480
So, he no longer needed the assistance of the&nbsp;
traitorous eight. You could say the traitorous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:14.940
eight got a taste of their own medicine but&nbsp;
not really. The problem with Sherman’s theory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:20.160
was that he was just straight up wrong. The&nbsp;
reality is that a lead within the semiconductor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:25.140
industry doesn’t mean anything. Moore’s law&nbsp;
suggests that the number of transistors in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:30.540
a circuit doubles every 2 years. There’s some&nbsp;
debate about whether this is still true today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:36.900
but it was more than true in the 1960s. In fact,&nbsp;
transistors were doubling every single year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:42.840
and the reason Moore’s law states every 2 years is&nbsp;
because Gordon was trying to be conservative. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:48.480
while Fairchild did have a massive lead, if they&nbsp;
slept for 1 year they would lose it all, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:53.040
that’s exactly what happened. As Sherman bought&nbsp;
out the stakes of the traitorous eight, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:59.640
would slowly leave 1 by 1, and by the end of the&nbsp;
1960s, they were all gone. From there, Fairchild&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:05.160
would begin its descent towards irrelevancy,&nbsp;
but our 8 heroes were far from being done.

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:10.020
UNBREAKABLE LEGACY:
Likely the two most notable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:20.340
men from the traitorous eight are Gordon&nbsp;
Moore and Robert Noyce. In 1968, this duo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:25.740
would go onto found Intel, you may have heard&nbsp;
of them. Ironically, their biggest competitors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:32.580
AMD was also founded by ex Fairchild employees.&nbsp;
In fact, it was created by Jerry Sanders and 7&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.580 --> 00:11:38.400
other ex Fairchild employees. You could say that&nbsp;
these were yet another traitorous 8. It doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:44.100
stop with just Intel and AMD though. Yet another&nbsp;
tech company created by these eight is Amelco.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:49.620
4 of the original eight: Jay Last, Jean Hoerni,&nbsp;
Eugene Kleiner, and Sheldon Roberts founded this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:54.540
company and it would eventually become modern&nbsp;
day Teledyne. You probably haven’t heard of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.540 --> 00:12:00.240
this company, but they’re worth $20 billion. Some&nbsp;
of the other members from the original 8 ended up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:04.920
taking another path. Eugene Kleiner for example&nbsp;
founded his own VC firm with the help of Tom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:11.820
Perkins. And these guys would go onto fund/create&nbsp;
Compaq, Intuit, Netscape, Sun Microsystems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.820 --> 00:12:17.400
Amazon, and dozens of other smaller companies.&nbsp;
In the meantime, another one of our heroes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:22.200
Victor Grinich, would go on to become a professor&nbsp;
at Stanford where he published a textbook called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:27.600
Introduction to Integrated Circuits. This textbook&nbsp;
would become the basis for how circuits are taught&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:33.420
in schools and universities around the world.&nbsp;
So, who knows how many tech geniuses this guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:38.940
inspired. Probably the least accomplished of&nbsp;
the 8 but still super accomplished is Julius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.940 --> 00:12:44.460
Blank. After Fairchild, he become a consultant for&nbsp;
startups and eventually retired in a retirement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.460 --> 00:12:49.920
home right across his office at Fairchild. Also,&nbsp;
if you’re wondering about Shockley, he never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:55.440
understood why these 8 left, but without a strong&nbsp;
team, he couldn’t really make much progress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.440 --> 00:13:01.200
and he would end up as a Stanford professor just&nbsp;
like his neighbor from his childhood. But, while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:07.740
Shockley never hit the mega jackpot himself and&nbsp;
though he had some rather odd perspectives, his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:14.280
contribution to the tech world is unquestionable.&nbsp;
It was from his little lab in the 1950s that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.280 --> 00:13:20.460
entirety of Silicon Valley was born and that’s the&nbsp;
story of the traitorous 8. Did you know about our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:26.640
heroes or I guess our anti heroes? Comment that&nbsp;
down below. Also, drop a like if you wanna hear&nbsp;&nbsp;

